  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA return to south winds is expected over the next 24 hours as\nthe mid level flow becomes zonal, encouraging lee troughing.\nHowever, an upper level trough traversing the northern plains\nwill push a weak front through Kansas early Saturday. But this\nfront will move back north as a warm front Saturday night as \nmoderate mid level westerly flow develops and again encourages \nsurface troughing in the lee of the Rockies. This pattern is \nfavorable for a low level warm plume edging downslope onto the \nhigh plains. As a result, temperatures will likely reach close \nto 100 for Sunday and Monday, after topping out in the upper \n80s to 90s for Friday and Saturday. The record highs at Dodge\nCity are 100 for both days.\n\nA small cooldown is possible by Tuesday and Wednesday as \nanother upper level trough traverses the northern plains and \npushes a weak cold front into Kansas. There are chances of \nt-storms along and behind the front Monday afternoon into \nTuesday. The best chance of t-storms is across central Kansas \nwhere the grand ensemble indicates 20-30% chances for .5\" or \ngreater of rain for the Monday night to Tuesday night time \nframe.\n\nTemperatures by mid to late next week have considerable\nuncertainty as the various ensemble suites differ on the\nstrength of the upper level ridge over the Deep South and\nsouthern plains. This also has implications on rain chances\ngiven that rich low level moisture would accompany any frontal\npassage.",
